WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Hi, I'm Luke Wroblewski, and I'm a Product Director at Google.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:12.000
I've spent over 20 years designing and building software that's used by billions of people worldwide.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:18.000
You might know me best for my work on mobile-first design. Back in 2011, I published a book called "Mobile First"

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.000
that changed how the industry thinks about responsive design. The core idea is simple but powerful:

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.000
design for mobile constraints first, then enhance for larger screens. This forces you to prioritize what truly matters.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.000
Before Google, I was CEO and co-founder of Polar, which Google acquired in 2014.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:42.000
Even before that, I co-founded Bagcheck, which Twitter acquired in 2011.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:48.000
My career has taken me through some amazing companies - I was Chief Design Architect at Yahoo,

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:54.000
Lead UI Designer at eBay, and Senior Interface Designer at NCSA, the birthplace of the Mosaic web browser.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:00.000
One thing I'm passionate about is metrics and measurement. I always tell teams:

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:06.000
"You are what you measure, so choose carefully." Here's my process for deciding what to track:

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:12.000
First, set clear goals. What do you actually want to see happen? Be specific.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Second, list potential metrics. These could be data you already have or need to start collecting.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:24.000
Third, for each metric, write down what would happen if you measure it. What actions would you take?

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:30.000
Fourth, rank your metrics by their impact on your goals. Which ones will drive the biggest, clearest outcomes?

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.000
Fifth, collect data for your top few metrics to see if they actually lead to the outcomes you expected.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:42.000
Finally, regularly and visibly track the ones that do work. Make them part of your team's DNA.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:48.000
I've also written two other books - "Web Form Design" and "Site-Seeing: A Visual Approach to Web Usability."

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:54.000
Forms might seem boring, but they're critical. Every transaction, every sign-up, every interaction often goes through a form.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:00.000
At Google, I've led product management, UX design, and user research for iOS and Android products.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.000
I also oversee metrics and analytics across departments. It's about connecting design decisions to business outcomes.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:12.000
My design philosophy centers on a few key principles. First, mobile-first thinking isn't just about screen size.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:18.000
It's about understanding context, constraints, and user behavior. People use mobile devices differently.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:24.000
Second, data should inform, not dictate, design. Numbers tell you what's happening, but you need to understand why.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:30.000
Third, simplicity scales better than complexity. When you're building for billions of users,

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.000
you need designs that are immediately understandable, regardless of technical literacy or cultural background.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:42.000
Fourth, focus on user goals, not features. Features are means to ends. What is the user trying to accomplish?

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:48.000
Fifth, test with real users early and often. Assumptions are dangerous. Validation is essential.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:54.000
When it comes to visual design, I believe in clarity and hierarchy. Users should instantly understand

00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:00.000
what's important, what's actionable, and what comes next. This is especially critical on small screens.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:06.000
I'm very active on LinkedIn, where I share insights with over 320,000 followers.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:12.000
I try to distill complex product and design concepts into practical, actionable advice.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:18.000
Looking back on my career, I'm proud that the mobile-first philosophy has become industry standard.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:24.000
It's changed how millions of designers and product teams think about creating digital experiences.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:30.000
My advice for aspiring UX designers? Start with constraints. Don't design for the ideal scenario.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:36.000
Design for the person with a slow connection, an older device, limited attention, and urgent needs.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:42.000
If your design works for them, it'll work beautifully for everyone else. That's the essence of mobile-first thinking.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:48.000
And always, always measure what matters. Let data guide your iterations, but let empathy guide your vision.
